  "meta": {
    "updated": "2026-07-28",
    "note": "Typical residential electricity prices per kWh. Country figures are average defaults; provider figures are indicative standard-tariff prices for the main retailers. All are editable in the calculators and vary by tariff, region, time of use and season.",
    "sources": "Sources: Eurostat dataset nrg_pc_204 for the EU and Norway; the US Energy Information Administration, Electric Power Monthly table 5.6.A, for the United States; Ofgem's energy price cap for Great Britain, unit rate plus standing charge spread over the band midpoint of 3,700 kWh/year so it is stated on the same all-in basis as Eurostat; GlobalPetrolPrices for Canada and Australia, which publishes a national residential price including generation, network charges and all taxes — no national regulator in either country publishes one, because electricity is regulated province by province and region by region. European Central Bank daily reference rates for the currency conversion. Retailer tariffs are indicative standard tariffs, not live quotes — no open API publishes them.",
    "sources_es": "Fuentes: el conjunto de datos nrg_pc_204 de Eurostat para la Unión Europea y Noruega; la Administración de Información Energética de Estados Unidos (EIA), Electric Power Monthly tabla 5.6.A, para Estados Unidos; el tope de precio de Ofgem para Gran Bretaña, término de energía más término fijo repartido sobre el punto medio de la banda, 3.700 kWh al año, para que quede expresado en la misma base con todo incluido que Eurostat; GlobalPetrolPrices para Canadá y Australia, que publica un precio residencial nacional con generación, peajes e impuestos incluidos — ningún regulador nacional de esos dos países publica uno, porque la electricidad se regula provincia a provincia y región a región. Tipos de cambio de referencia diarios del Banco Central Europeo para la conversión de moneda. Las tarifas de las comercializadoras son tarifas estándar indicativas, no ofertas en vivo: ninguna API abierta las publica.",
    "co2Note": "co2 = national grid carbon intensity in grams CO2-equivalent per kWh, lifecycle basis (annual average generation mix). 2025 figures from Our World in Data / Ember's Global Electricity Review 2026 — see wattfigures.com/legal/methodology/.",
    "gasNote": "gas = residential natural gas price per kWh of gas burned, in the same local currency as price, for the countries where an open source covers it. Europe: Eurostat nrg_pc_202, household band D2 (20-199 GJ/year), all taxes included — the same semester and the same tax basis as the electricity figure, so the two can be compared directly. United States: EIA Monthly Energy Review table 9.10, residential delivered price, averaged over the last complete November-March heating season and converted at 1 Mcf = 1.037 MMBtu. The heating season and not the year because a residential per-unit gas price is mostly standing charge divided by volume: it more than doubles in summer when nobody is heating, and the pages that use this figure compare heating systems. Three decimals because gas lands near 0.05 and two would quantise it by 2%.",
    "definition": "Country prices are the average residential price per kWh with ALL TAXES INCLUDED, for the 2500-4999 kWh/year consumption band (a typical medium household). One definition for every country: mixing tax-inclusive and tax-exclusive figures is what put six countries out by 15% or more until 2026-07-26.",
    "period": "2025-S2",
    "fxDate": "2026-07-27"
  },
